WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:06.060
Speaker 1: Tomorrow's arrest, surrender and
arraignment of Donald Trump.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:09.620
Is of paramount public interest.

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:19.230
This is a former president of the United States,
now indicted for crimes related to his candidacy

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:21.000
in 2016.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:28.340
He is being prosecuted by the taxpayer funded
legal system and is the epitome of a public

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:31.480
figure, never mind just nationally, but globally.

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:34.320
The arraignment should be televised.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:35.600
But will it be?

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:37.280
We do not yet know.

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:43.640
It is imperative that there be cameras in
the courtroom tomorrow, not only for the reasons

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:50.000
I just said, but also because, of course,
Trump is going to lie about what happens in

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:51.370
this proceeding.

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:57.280
He will say the judge was so unfair and the
prosecutors and everybody is just biased.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:03.899
And one of the only defenses we have against
those lies is to actually have cameras showing

00:01:03.899 --> 00:01:05.500
what is going on.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:12.600
The New York Times Maggie Haberman appeared
on CNN and made the exact same argument.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:13.600
Let's take a.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:14.600
Speaker 2: Listen.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:15.600
I mean, what do you think?

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:19.490
I mean, if there is a camera in the room,
that's a remarkable moment in and of itself.

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:23.380
But it could be important to actually get
a clear view of what happened in that that

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:24.380
courtroom.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:25.380
Absolutely.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:26.930
And there is a compelling public interest
argument to make.

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:30.700
It is absolutely true that New York New York
is really regressive in terms of cameras in

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:31.700
the courtroom.

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:33.100
I do expect there'll be an argument made.

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:35.790
I'm not sure what the ruling will be on that.

00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:38.900
But it's interesting, I was thinking as you
were asking that question and one of the things

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:43.600
we saw over and over, both with Michael Cohen
in terms of Trump and then other people in

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:47.189
the White House, people have a habit of taping
Donald Trump and they have a habit of taping

00:01:47.189 --> 00:01:52.149
Donald Trump because he spends his own reality
and will contradict whatever people say about

00:01:52.149 --> 00:01:53.149
him.

00:01:53.149 --> 00:01:58.080
A camera would be pretty vital in terms of
people understanding what happened and not

00:01:58.080 --> 00:01:59.899
being told that something that's fake news.

00:01:59.899 --> 00:02:03.460
And in an era where Trump is saying that all
of these reports are not true.

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:05.380
I think there is a compelling argument for
it.

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:07.190
I am skeptical that it will happen.

00:02:07.190 --> 00:02:08.989
Just I covered courts in this state.

00:02:08.989 --> 00:02:12.520
I know how against cameras in the courtroom
judges are here.

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:14.209
But I do think there is going to be a compelling
argument.

00:02:14.209 --> 00:02:15.209
Speaker 1: And.

00:02:15.209 --> 00:02:17.860
Speaker 3: Even toggling to the actual trial
itself.

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:21.379
Imagine what the public interest in that will
be.

00:02:21.379 --> 00:02:25.960
And so will there be cameras allowed to depict
what occurs on the day to day?

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:28.459
Because I think certainly people would want
to know right now.

00:02:28.459 --> 00:02:32.660
Speaker 1: I don't know whether there will
be cameras in the courtroom tomorrow.

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:39.129
I sympathize with the skepticism from Maggie
Haberman, because indeed, oftentimes there's

00:02:39.129 --> 00:02:42.060
questions, why weren't there cameras in the
courtroom?

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:46.850
And sometimes it has to do with, well, it's
a federal court rather than a state court,

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:48.890
and there are different procedures there.

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:54.220
New York State also notoriously strict about
having cameras in the courtroom.

00:02:54.220 --> 00:03:01.489
I can't think of any legal issue, any legal
proceeding where cameras in the courtroom

00:03:01.489 --> 00:03:07.250
are more justified than the indictment of
a former president of the United States.

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:11.989
So by tomorrow, we will probably know more
to livestreams tomorrow, the arraignment in

00:03:11.989 --> 00:03:17.150
the afternoon and the press conference at
Mar a Lago, if it's not canceled based on

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:19.319
a potentially forthcoming gag order.

00:03:19.319 --> 00:03:20.883
It is going to be a crazy 24, 36 hours.

